<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Thunderbird_(John_Proudstar)> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Thunderbird** (**John Proudstar**) is a fictional [superhero](:Superhero) appearing in [American comic books](:American_comic_book) published by [Marvel Comics](:Marvel_Comics). Created by writer [Len Wein](:Len_Wein) and artist [Dave Cockrum](:Dave_Cockrum), the character first appears in *[Giant-Size X-Men](:Giant-Size_X-Men)* #1 (May 1975). Thunderbird was a short-lived member of the Second Genesis group of [X-Men](:X-Men) gathered together in this issue, as he died on their second mission.An [Apache](:Apache) [Native American](:Native_Americans_in_the_United_States) and [Human Mutant](:Mutant_%28Marvel_Comics%29), John Proudstar possesses superhuman athletic ability. Since his death, the character has been temporarily brought back to life in the [Necrosha](:Necrosha) and [Chaos War](:Chaos_War) storylines. His brother, [James Proudstar](:Warpath_%28comics%29), known first as Thunderbird, and then as Warpath, is also a mutant and X-Men with similar capabilities.In addition to his [mainstream](:Marvel_Universe) incarnations, Thunderbird has been depicted in other [fictional universes](:Fictional_universes). The most notable alternative version of the character is a member of the original [Exiles](:Exiles_%28Marvel_Comics%29) team. In other media, Thunderbird is one of the main characters adapted to the [live-action](:Live_action) television series *[The Gifted](:The_Gifted_%28American_TV_series%29)*, which debuted in 2017, portrayed by the actor [Blair Redford](:Blair_Redford)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Mala_(Kryptonian)> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q24229398> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Alan_Grant_(writer)> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Alan Grant (9 February 1949 – 20 July 2022) was a British comic book writer known for writing Judge Dredd in 2000 AD as well as various Batman titles from the late 1980s to the early 2000s. He was the co-creator of the characters Anarky, Victor Zsasz, and the Ventriloquist."@en .
