<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Len_Wein> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Leonard Norman Wein (/wiːn/; June 12, 1948 – September 10, 2017) was an American comic book writer and editor best known for co-creating DC Comics' Swamp Thing and Marvel Comics' Wolverine, and for helping revive the Marvel superhero team the X-Men (including the co-creation of Nightcrawler, Storm, and Colossus). Additionally, he was the editor for writer Alan Moore and illustrator Dave Gibbons' influential DC miniseries Watchmen. Wein was inducted into the Will Eisner Comic Book Hall of Fame in 2008."@en .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Jack_Kirby> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#differentFrom> <http://dbpedia.org/resource/Josh_Kirby> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Nagraj> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Nagraj** is a [superhero](:Superhero) appearing in [Indian comic books](:Indian_comics) published by [Raj Comics](:Raj_Comics) created in the late 1980s by Rajkumar Gupta. Nagraj first appeared in the comics Nagraj GENL #14 which was written by Parshuram Sharma and illustrated by [Pratap Mullick](:Pratap_Mullick).After that Sanjay Ashtpure, Pratap Mullick, Chandu, Milind Misal and Vitthal Kamble alternately illustrated the character for 44 issues, ending in 1995 with Visarpi Ki Shadi.Nagraj is believed to have been inspired by the mythological *[Ichchhadhari Nag](:Ichchhadhari_Nag)* (shapeshifting snakes) and historical *Vishmanushya* (venomous human). His stories create a rich blend of mythology, fantasy, magic, and science fiction. Many of Nagraj's fans believe that, over time, Nagraj's comics have developed snake mythology of its own, which is unique to the popular Indian beliefs about snakes that are prevalent among the masses.In the middle of the nineties [Anupam Sinha](:Anupam_Sinha) (who was already running the mega-successful [Dhruv](:Super_Commando_Dhruva) series) took over the helms of Nagraj as well. Anupam Sinha's expansive narration could not completely fit in a single comic, so he used multi-comic stories to layer and enrich his tales. The Khajana arc by Anupam Sinha set up the backstory beautifully, and discredited Nagmani many years after the comics began. He also led to increase in frequency of Two-in-One comics, which combined Nagraj and Dhruv and started happening once in a year. Nagraj has changed a lot in his 25 years in print, both in terms of looks as well as powers and abilities. With time many new artists such as Lalit Sharma and Hemant Kumar have done illustration of Nagraj."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/John_Romita_Jr.> <http://www.w3.org/ns/dcat#resource_identifier> "0,45461"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#double> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Paul_Neary> <http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/name> "Paul Neary"@en .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Julius_Schwartz> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Julius** \"**Julie**\" **Schwartz** (; June 19, 1915 – February 8, 2004) was a [comic book](:Comic_book) [editor](:Editing), and a [science fiction](:Science_fiction) [agent](:Literary_agent) and prominent [fan](:Fan_%28aficionado%29). He was born in [The Bronx](:The_Bronx), [New York](:New_York_%28state%29). He is best known as a longtime editor at [DC Comics](:DC_Comics), where at various times he was primary editor over the company's flagship [superheroes](:Superheroes), [Superman](:Superman) and [Batman](:Batman).He was inducted into the comics industry's [Jack Kirby Hall of Fame](:List_of_Harvey_Award_winners%23The_Jack_Kirby_Hall_of_Fame) in 1996 and the [Will Eisner Comic Book Hall of Fame](:List_of_Eisner_Award_winners%23The_Will_Eisner_Award_Hall_of_Fame) in 1997."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
