<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Brian_Michael_Bendis> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Brian Michael Bendis** (; born August 18, 1967) is an [American](:Americans) [comic book](:Comic_book) writer and artist. He has won five [Eisner Awards](:Eisner_Award) for both his creator-owned work and his work on various [Marvel Comics](:Marvel_Comics) books.Starting with [crime](:Crime_fiction) and [noir](:Hardboiled) comics, Bendis eventually moved to mainstream [superhero](:Superhero) work. With [Bill Jemas](:Bill_Jemas) and [Mark Millar](:Mark_Millar), Bendis was the writer on the first book of the [Ultimate Marvel](:Ultimate_Marvel) Universe, launching *[Ultimate Spider-Man](:Ultimate_Spider-Man)* in 2000. He relaunched the [Avengers](:Avengers_%28comics%29) franchise with *[New Avengers](:The_New_Avengers_%28comics%29)* in 2004, and also wrote the Marvel storylines \"[Secret War](:Secret_War_%28comics%29)\" (2004–2005), \"[House of M](:House_of_M)\" (2005), \"[Secret Invasion](:Secret_Invasion)\" (2008), \"[Siege](:Siege_%28comics%29)\" (2010) and \"[Age of Ultron](:Age_of_Ultron)\" (2013).Though Bendis has cited comic book writers such as [Frank Miller](:Frank_Miller) and [Alan Moore](:Alan_Moore), his own writing influences are less rooted in comics, drawing on the work of [David Mamet](:David_Mamet), [Richard Price](:Richard_Price_%28writer%29), and [Aaron Sorkin](:Aaron_Sorkin), whose dialogue, Bendis said, was \"the best in any medium.\"In addition to writing comics, Bendis has worked in television, video games and film. He has also taught courses on [graphic novels](:Graphic_novel) at [University of Oregon](:University_of_Oregon) and [Portland State University](:Portland_State_University). In 2014, Bendis wrote *Words for Pictures: The Art and Business of Writing Comics and Graphic Novels,* a book about comics published by [Random House](:Random_House)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
