<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Ted_Cowan> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Ted Cowan, being the best known familiar name of Edward George Cowan, was a British comic book writer. His early career included working as a laboratory assistant prior to World War II when he enlisted firstly in the Royal Air Force and subsequently in the British Army where he was a dispatch rider. After the war he initially continued in the Services in a clerical capacity when he began to make the change to writing. His Tarzanesque jungle story Saber King of the Jungle for Tiger was later reprised in France as Yataca. Cowan died in the early 1970s."@en .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Dick_Sprang> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Thing> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Dan_Turner,_Hollywood_Detective> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Dan Turner**, also known as the **Hollywood Detective**, was a fictional [private detective](:Private_investigator) created by [Robert Leslie Bellem](:Robert_Leslie_Bellem). His first appearance was in the second issue of the [pulp magazine](:Pulp_magazine) *Spicy Detective*, dated June 1934, and he continued to appear regularly in that magazine (which was retitled *Speed Detective* in 1943) until its demise in February 1947. He also appeared in his \"own\" magazine, *Hollywood Detective*, which was published by Culture Publications (later Trojan Publishing) and ran from January 1942 to October 1950."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/John_Wagner> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://dbpedia.org/ontology/ComicsCreator> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Peter_Milligan> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Peter Milligan** (born 24 June 1961) is a British [comic book writer](:Comic_book_writer) who has written extensively for both British and American comic book industries. In the UK, Milligan has contributed to numerous anthology titles including *[2000 AD](:2000_AD_%28comics%29)*, *[Revolver](:Revolver_%28Fleetway_comics%29)*, *[Eagle](:Eagle_%28British_comics%29)* and *[A1](:A1_%28comics%29)*, and helped launch the influential magazine *[Deadline](:Deadline_%28magazine%29)*. In the US, he is best known for his frequent contributions to [DC Comics](:DC_Comics)' [Vertigo](:Vertigo_%28DC_Comics%29) imprint, which include the revamped DC properties *[Shade, the Changing Man](:Shade%2C_the_Changing_Man_%28Vertigo%29)* and *[Human Target](:Human_Target_%28Vertigo%29)*, a four-year run on the imprint's premier title *[Hellblazer](:Hellblazer)*, and original series *[Enigma](:Enigma_%28Vertigo%29)*, *[The Extremist](:The_Extremist_%28comics%29)*, *[Egypt](:Egypt_%28comics%29)* and *[Greek Street](:Greek_Street_%28comics%29)*, as well as the [Marvel](:Marvel_Comics) series *[X-Statix](:X-Statix)*, co-created by Milligan and artist [Mike Allred](:Mike_Allred)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
