<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Turk_Barrett> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.ontologydesignpatterns.org/ont/dul/DUL.owl#Agent> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Northwestern_University> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3918> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Hypnota> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Hypnota** (also sometimes **Hypnotic Woman**) is a fictional character appearing in [DC Comics](:DC_Comics) publications and related media, commonly as a recurring adversary of the superhero [Wonder Woman](:Wonder_Woman). Created by writer [William Moulton Marston](:William_Moulton_Marston) and artist [Harry G. Peter](:H%2EG%2E_Peter), the character debuted in 1944 in *[Wonder Woman](:Wonder_Woman_%28comic_book%29)* (volume 1) #11 as a stage magician and human trafficker with powerful superhuman mind-control abilities. The gender presentation of her stage persona, Hypnota the Great, was that of an ostensibly male figure in [Orientalized](:Orientalism) Middle-Eastern costume, complete with a false mustache and goatee. Though initially appearing to disguise her gender to deflect criminal suspicion (a [genderplay](:Genderplay) trope Marston incorporated into several other foes he created to battle Wonder Woman, including [Doctor Poison](:Doctor_Poison) and the [Blue Snowman](:Blue_Snowman%29), Hypnota made subsequent [Golden Age](:Golden_Age_of_Comics) appearances in her masculine stage garb; even after her supposedly \"true\" gender identity was revealed, she chose to present as a man – a move that might be understood in the 21st century as [genderqueer](:Non-binary_gender). The [Modern Age](:Modern_Age_of_Comics) Hypnota, renamed Hypnotic Woman, has abandoned her false facial hair and is now written and drawn as a [cisgender](:Cisgender) woman, albeit one who wears a somewhat masculine costume similar to her Golden Age look: a closed-front vest, [salwar](:Salwar) and a man's [turban](:Turban)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
